# Professional Animation System Fix

The "jumping" behavior is caused by specialized animations (like `VectorOperation`) overwriting the global `transitionDuration` with a very small value (16ms) to force frame updates. This makes all other objects in the scene snap to their final state instantly.

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> I am splitting the transition state into "Beat Transitions" (global) and "Specialized Animations" (object-specific). This ensures that while a vector is growing, other elements still slide smoothly at their own intended speed.

## Proposed Changes

### Rendering Engine

#### [MODIFY] [canvas-renderer.ts](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/lectvideoanalyser/MathViz/src/renderers/canvas-renderer.ts)
- **State Refaming**:
    - Rename `transitionStartTime` to `beatStartTime`.
    - Rename `transitionDuration` to `beatDuration`.
    - Add `lastFrameTime` to track frame-to-frame updates for specialized animators.
- **Improved Render Loop**:
    - The loop will now calculate `t` using `beatDuration` for all standard object state transitions.
    - Specialized animators (like `animateVectorOperation`) will now simply add to `activeAnimations` to keep the loop alive, without poisoning the `beatDuration`.
    - The loop will continue running as long as `(now - beatStartTime < beatDuration)` OR `activeAnimations.size > 0`.
- **Refactor `animateVectorOperation` and friends**:
    - Remove the code that sets `state.transitionDuration = 16`.
    - Ensure they correctly cleanup `activeAnimations`.

